# CSE450 Translation of Programming Languages
## Lecture 12: Flow Control in WebAssembly
---
% PowerPoint slide 2.
### What Is Flow Control?
[definition] @(70, 150) {$note, w=1140px, size=31px} **Flow control** changes which instruction executes next.
[return] @(90, 265) {$note, w=1100px, size=29px} **{{color=emp|return}}** exits a function and supplies its result.
...
[if] @(90, 340) {$note, w=1100px, size=29px} **{{color=emp|if / else}}** chooses a statement based on a condition.
...
[while] @(90, 415) {$note, w=1100px, size=29px} **{{color=emp|while}}** repeats a statement while its condition is true.
...
[break] @(90, 490) {$note, w=1100px, size=29px} **{{color=emp|break}}** exits the innermost enclosing loop.
[continue] @(90, 565) {$note, w=1100px, size=29px} **{{color=emp|continue}}** jumps to that loop's condition.
---
### Project 3 Stack Contracts
[expr] @(70, 165) {$note, w=1140px, size=31px} **An expression leaves one `f64` value.** Every Strix source value is a `double`, including comparison results.
...
[stmt] @(70, 295) {$note, w=1140px, size=31px} **A statement leaves no value when it finishes normally.** An expression statement uses `drop` to discard its result.
...
[cond] @(70, 445) {$note, w=1140px, size=31px} **Wasm `if` and `br_if` consume an `i32` condition.** Convert the source value at this boundary.
[return] @(70, 590) {$note, w=1140px, size=27px, color=explain} `return` transfers its value to the caller. It does not resume the next statement in this function.
---
% PowerPoint slides 4-5.
### Implementing return
[wat] @(70, 150) {$code, w=765px, size=23px} ```wat
(module
  (func $add
    (param $var0 f64) (param $var1 f64)
    (result f64)
    (local $var2 f64)

    (local.get $var0)
    (local.get $var1)
    (f64.add)
    (local.set $var2)

    (local.get $var2)
    (return)
  )
  (export "Add" (func $add))
)
```
[setup] @(870, 165) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=explain} Declare parameters, the f64 result, and all locals before body instructions.
...
[value] @(870, 345) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=emp} First generate the return expression. Then emit `(return)`.
...
[exit] @(870, 510) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=explain} This exits the whole function, even from inside an if or loop.
---
% PowerPoint slide 7, corrected to satisfy the current final-return rule.
### The Final Return in Strix
[source] @(70, 175) {$code, w=790px, size=25px} ```cpp
function Max(double v1, double v2) : double {
  if (v1 > v2) return v1;
  return v2;
}
```
[early] @(905, 180) {$note, w=295px, size=27px, color=explain} The first return is conditional. The final return handles the other case.
...
[rule] @(70, 395) {$note, w=1140px, size=30px, color=emp} Each function body must end with a direct `return expression;` statement.
...
[both] @(70, 520) {$note, w=1140px, size=28px} Even if both branches of an if/else return, Project 3 still requires a final direct return after the if/else.
[why] @(70, 630) {$note, w=1140px, size=25px, color=id} This is a simple source-language rule. Wasm itself does not impose it.
---
% PowerPoint slide 6.
### Implementing if / else
[wat] @(70, 150) {$code, w=765px, size=25px} ```wat
;; Generate the source condition: f64
(f64.const 0)
(f64.ne)              ;; Convert to i32
(if
  (then
    ;; Generate the then statement
  )
  (else
    ;; Generate the else statement
  )
)
```
[explain] @(870, 170) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=explain} The condition selects exactly one branch.
...
[optional] @(870, 310) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=explain} Omit the `(else ...)` when the source has no else.
...
[result] @(870, 455) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=emp} A statement if needs no `(result f64)`. Its branches leave no value on normal completion.
[template] @(70, 640) {$note, w=1140px, size=25px, color=id} Translation template: replace the comments with generated instructions.
---
### Converting a Strix Condition
[wat] @(70, 170) {$code, w=730px, size=28px} ```wat
;; Source expression leaves an f64
(f64.const 0)
(f64.ne)          ;; i32: value != 0
```
[zero] @(70, 360) {$note, w=730px, size=30px} **False:** zero, including negative zero.
[other] @(70, 440) {$note, w=730px, size=30px} **True:** every other value, including fractions, infinities, and NaN.
...
[pitfall] @(860, 180) {$note, w=340px, size=29px, color=focus} Why would integer truncation give the wrong answer for 0.5?
...
[answer] @(860, 370) {$note, w=340px, size=28px, color=emp} It becomes 0. Comparing with zero preserves the required truth value.
[not] @(70, 605) {$note, w=1140px, size=26px} For source `!x`: compare x with zero using `f64.eq`, then use `f64.convert_i32_s` to produce a source double.
---
### Comparison Results Are Also Doubles
[wat] @(70, 170) {$code, w=765px, size=25px} ```wat
;; Source expression: v1 > v2
(local.get $var0)
(local.get $var1)
(f64.gt)             ;; i32: 0 or 1
(f64.convert_i32_s)   ;; f64: 0.0 or 1.0
```
[value] @(870, 175) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=explain} A comparison is an expression. Its value may be stored, returned, or used in arithmetic.
...
[cond] @(70, 425) {$code, w=765px, size=25px} ```wat
;; Use that source value as a condition
(f64.const 0)
(f64.ne)             ;; Back to i32
```
[opt] @(870, 425) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=emp} Optional optimization: when used only as a condition, keep the comparison's i32 directly.
[contract] @(70, 620) {$note, w=1140px, size=26px, color=id} The general translation preserves the same expression contract everywhere.
---
% PowerPoint slide 8, using the current source-expression contract.
### Translating Max
[wat] @(70, 145) {$code, w=780px, size=22px} ```wat
(module
  (func $max
    (param $var0 f64) (param $var1 f64) (result f64)
    (local.get $var0)
    (local.get $var1)
    (f64.gt)
    (f64.convert_i32_s)
    (f64.const 0)
    (f64.ne)
    (if
      (then
        (return (local.get $var0)))
    )
    (return (local.get $var1))
  )
  (export "Max" (func $max))
)
```
[cond] @(890, 165) {$note, w=310px, size=26px, color=explain} Generate v1 > v2 as a double, then convert it to the condition consumed by if.
...
[then] @(890, 355) {$note, w=310px, size=26px, color=emp} If true, return v1 immediately.
...
[final] @(890, 475) {$note, w=310px, size=26px, color=explain} Otherwise, reach the final return of v2.
[calls] @(890, 605) {$note, w=310px, size=25px} `Max(3, 8)` gives 8.{{br}}`Max(9, 2)` gives 9.
---
% PowerPoint slide 9.
### Implementing while
[wat] @(70, 150) {$code, w=765px, size=23px} ```wat
(block $exit1
  (loop $loop1
    ;; Generate condition: one f64
    (f64.const 0)
    (f64.ne)         ;; i32 truth value
    (i32.eqz)        ;; Is it false?
    (br_if $exit1)

    ;; Generate the loop body

    (br $loop1)
  )
)
```
[exit] @(870, 160) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=explain} Branching to the outer block resumes after the block.
...
[repeat] @(870, 315) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=explain} Branching to the loop restarts at its beginning, so the condition runs again.
...
[once] @(870, 515) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=focus} A Wasm loop repeats only when code branches back to it.
[template] @(70, 640) {$note, w=1140px, size=25px, color=id} Translation template: the body must leave no value on normal completion.
---
% PowerPoint slides 10-14: factorial source, condition, and assignment builds.
### Example while: Factorial
[source] @(70, 165) {$code, w=790px, size=25px} ```cpp
function Factorial(double n) : double {
  double result = 1;
  double i = 2;
  while (i <= n) {
    result = result * i;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```
[vars] @(905, 175) {$note, w=295px, size=27px, color=id} `n`: parameter `$var0`{{br}}`result`: local `$var1`{{br}}`i`: local `$var2`
...
[predict] @(905, 370) {$note, w=295px, size=27px, color=focus} What does Factorial return for 0, 1, and 5?
...
[answer] @(905, 525) {$note, w=295px, size=28px, color=emp} 1, 1, and 120.
[domain] @(70, 620) {$note, w=1140px, size=26px} Use nonnegative integers for this factorial example. Strix still represents n as a double.
---
### Factorial: Testing the Loop Condition
[wat] @(70, 150) {$code, w=765px, size=24px} ```wat
(local.get $var2)     ;; i
(local.get $var0)     ;; n
(f64.le)             ;; i32: i <= n
(f64.convert_i32_s)   ;; Source double
(f64.const 0)
(f64.ne)             ;; i32 condition
(i32.eqz)            ;; Condition is false
(br_if $exit1)
```
[generic] @(870, 165) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=explain} This follows the general expression and condition rules.
...
[short] @(70, 470) {$code, w=765px, size=23px} ```wat
;; Optional: comparison used only as a test
(local.get $var2)
(local.get $var0)
(f64.le)
(i32.eqz)
(br_if $exit1)
```
[nan] @(870, 450) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=focus} Keep the logical inversion. Replacing it with f64.gt changes behavior when an operand is NaN.
---
### Factorial: Assignment Statements
[source] @(70, 145) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} Source statement: `result = result * i;`
[baseline] @(70, 225) {$code, w=765px, size=26px} ```wat
(local.get $var1)
(local.get $var2)
(f64.mul)
(local.set $var1)
(local.get $var1)     ;; Assignment value
(drop)               ;; Expression statement
```
[explain] @(870, 230) {$note, w=330px, size=27px, color=explain} Assignment leaves the stored value. The enclosing statement discards it.
...
!anim baseline hide 0.15s
[tee] @(70, 225) {$code, w=765px, size=26px} ```wat
(local.get $var1)
(local.get $var2)
(f64.mul)
(local.tee $var1)     ;; Store and retain
(drop)               ;; Expression statement
```
!anim explain text local.tee combines the set and get. This is a convenient general assignment translation.
...
!anim tee hide 0.15s
[optimized] @(70, 225) {$code, w=765px, size=26px} ```wat
(local.get $var1)
(local.get $var2)
(f64.mul)
(local.set $var1)     ;; Store, no stack value
```
!anim explain text When the value is discarded immediately, local.set is sufficient. This optimization is optional.
[increment] @(70, 560) {$note, w=1140px, size=28px, color=emp} Apply the same reasoning to `i = i + 1;`.
[warning] @(70, 630) {$note, w=1140px, size=25px} Preserve the value when assignment is used inside a larger expression.
---
### Factorial: The Complete WAT Function
[wat] @(70, 150) {$code, w=835px, size=19px, pad=8px} ```wat
(module
  (func $factorial (param $var0 f64) (result f64)
    (local $var1 f64) (local $var2 f64)
    (local.set $var1 (f64.const 1))
    (local.set $var2 (f64.const 2))
    (block $exit1
      (loop $loop1
        (br_if $exit1 (i32.eqz
          (f64.le (local.get $var2) (local.get $var0))))
        (local.set $var1
          (f64.mul (local.get $var1) (local.get $var2)))
        (local.set $var2
          (f64.add (local.get $var2) (f64.const 1)))
        (br $loop1)
      )
    )
    (return (local.get $var1))
  )
  (export "Factorial" (func $factorial))
)
```
[folded] @(945, 160) {$note, w=255px, size=25px, color=explain} Folded instructions place operand expressions inside the instruction that uses them.
...
[optimized] @(945, 365) {$note, w=255px, size=25px, color=emp} This version uses the optional comparison and assignment simplifications.
[same] @(945, 560) {$note, w=255px, size=25px} The general translation is also correct.
---
% PowerPoint slides 15-19, consolidated into reveal steps.
### Implementing break and continue
[context] @(70, 155) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} Inside `(block $exit1 (loop $loop1 ...))`, which branch implements each statement?
[breaklabel] @(70, 300) {$note, w=510px, size=32px, color=focus} `break;`
[continuelabel] @(680, 300) {$note, w=510px, size=32px, color=focus} `continue;`
...
[breakcode] @(70, 375) {$code, w=510px, size=29px} ```wat
(br $exit1)
```
[breaknote] @(70, 490) {$note, w=510px, size=28px} Leave the loop and resume after the outer block.
...
[continuecode] @(680, 375) {$code, w=510px, size=29px} ```wat
(br $loop1)
```
[continuenote] @(680, 490) {$note, w=510px, size=28px} Skip the rest of this iteration and recheck the condition.
[return] @(70, 630) {$note, w=1140px, size=26px, color=emp} `return` exits the function, regardless of how many loops enclose it.
---
### Nested Loops Need a Stack of Labels
[wat] @(70, 155) {$code, w=765px, size=23px} ```wat
(block $exit1
  (loop $loop1
    ;; Outer condition and body...
    (block $exit2
      (loop $loop2
        ;; Inner condition and body...
        ;; Inner break:    (br $exit2)
        ;; Inner continue: (br $loop2)
      )
    )
    ;; Outer break:    (br $exit1)
    ;; Outer continue: (br $loop1)
  )
)
```
[push] @(870, 165) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=explain} Before translating a loop body, push its exit and repeat labels.
...
[use] @(870, 325) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=emp} Translate break and continue using the top pair. An if does not change that pair.
...
[pop] @(870, 510) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=explain} Pop the pair after translating the body. Give each loop distinct labels.
[template] @(70, 650) {$note, w=1140px, size=24px, color=id} Nesting sketch: condition checks and ordinary iteration branches are omitted.
---
### Example: Skipping One Iteration
[source] @(70, 150) {$code, w=790px, size=23px} ```cpp
function SumExceptThree(double limit) : double {
  double sum = 0;
  double i = 0;
  while (i < limit) {
    i = i + 1;
    if (i == 3) continue;
    sum = sum + i;
  }
  return sum;
}
```
[predict] @(905, 170) {$note, w=295px, size=27px, color=focus} What does the host call SumExceptThree(5) return?
...
[answer] @(905, 325) {$note, w=295px, size=27px, color=emp} 12: it adds 1 + 2 + 4 + 5.
...
[break] @(70, 545) {$note, w=1140px, size=28px} Replace `continue` with `break`: the result becomes 3, from 1 + 2.
[progress] @(70, 635) {$note, w=1140px, size=25px, color=explain} Updating i before continue ensures the next condition sees the new value.
---
### Semantic Checks for Flow Control
[loop] @(70, 160) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} **Loop context:** break and continue require an enclosing while. Track loop depth during semantic traversal.
...
[return] @(70, 285) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} **Returns:** require the function body's last statement to be a direct return. Analyze its expression normally.
...
[unreachable] @(70, 410) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px} **All source code:** validate both branches and unreachable statements. A return does not end semantic traversal.
...
[error] @(70, 540) {$note, w=1140px, size=28px, color=emp} Invalid source: report `ERROR (line N): ...` on stderr, return status 1, and leave stdout empty.
[order] @(70, 640) {$note, w=1140px, size=25px, color=id} Parse and check the complete program before emitting its WAT module.
---
### Source Scope and Wasm Locals
[source] @(70, 160) {$code, w=765px, size=24px} ```cpp
function ScopeExample() : double {
  double x = 10;
  {
    double x = x + 1;
    x = 20;
  }
  return x;
}
```
[ids] @(870, 170) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=explain} Each declaration gets a distinct local ID. Both locals appear at the start of the WAT function.
...
[init] @(870, 365) {$note, w=330px, size=26px, color=emp} Resolve the initializer before declaring the inner x. The function returns 10.
...
[braces] @(70, 500) {$note, w=1140px, size=28px} Only braces introduce scopes. An unbraced if/while declaration belongs to the surrounding scope.
[runtime] @(70, 610) {$note, w=1140px, size=26px} Locals start at zero on each call. A declaration's initializer runs whenever execution reaches it, including each loop iteration.
---
% PowerPoint slides 3 and 20.
### Other Forms of Flow Control
[loops] @(70, 155) {$note, w=1140px, size=28px} **Other loops:** for adds initialization and an update, foreach visits a container, do/while tests at the end, and repeat/until stops when its test is true.
...
[choices] @(70, 285) {$note, w=1140px, size=28px} **Other choices:** switch selects a case, a ternary operator selects a value, and a jump transfers control to a target.
...
[calls] @(70, 405) {$note, w=1140px, size=28px} **Calls and suspension:** function calls run another function, yield suspends execution, and try/catch handles exceptional control flow.
...
[question] @(70, 540) {$note, w=1140px, size=29px, color=focus} How could we adapt the block/loop pattern for a for loop? Where should continue go?
[scope] @(70, 645) {$note, w=1140px, size=24px, color=id} Beyond Project 3. Calls between Strix functions begin in Project 4. The host can call exports now.
---
% PowerPoint slide 21: the compiler structure, mapped to current starter files.
### Where Flow Control Fits in the Compiler
[front] @(70, 155) {$note, w=550px, size=31px, color=emp} **Front End**
[lex] @(70, 225) {$note, w=550px, size=27px} Lexical analysis recognizes keywords and punctuation.
[parse] @(70, 325) {$note, w=550px, size=27px} `Parser.hpp` builds AST nodes and resolves names.
[check] @(70, 425) {$note, w=550px, size=27px} `Semantics.hpp` checks the AST, including loop context and returns.
...
[back] @(680, 155) {$note, w=520px, size=31px, color=emp} **Back End**
[ir] @(680, 225) {$note, w=520px, size=27px} The AST is our intermediate representation. Later compilers may add more IRs and optimization passes.
[gen] @(680, 365) {$note, w=520px, size=27px} `Translate.hpp` visits the AST and generates WAT instructions and labels.
[target] @(680, 490) {$note, w=520px, size=27px} `wat2wasm` assembles the WAT into the target Wasm binary.
[ast] @(70, 620) {$note, w=1140px, size=25px, color=id} `ASTKind::If`: condition, then, optional else. `ASTKind::While`: condition, body.
